Hamnix Micro feather-nrf52840 size patch to user/hamnix/ls.ad (the committed file plus the teensy41 patch's changes, which it repeats).
Applied by scripts/mkroot.sh in build/feather-nrf52840/root only; the
committed file is unchanged.

Reason: the nRF52840 has 256 KiB of RAM for everything: kernel data, kernel
stacks and heap, the files in / and every running program's stack and data
(boards/feather_nrf52840/link.ld; programs' code stays in flash).
    listing buffer    64 KiB -> 4 KiB (truncation is reported)

Only constants, array lengths, and messages that quote them change.
Generated by editing a copy and
    diff -u --label a/user/hamnix/ls.ad --label b/user/hamnix/ls.ad user/hamnix/ls.ad <copy>

--- a/user/hamnix/ls.ad
+++ b/user/hamnix/ls.ad
@@ -47,7 +47,7 @@
 # undercount. Same class as the hamsh argv/glob caps fixed 2026-07-28. 64 KiB
 # holds ~8000 short names, and a FULL buffer is now reported on stderr with a
 # nonzero exit rather than passed off as a complete listing.
-LISTBUF:  uint64 = 65536
+LISTBUF:  uint64 = 4096
 
 
 def strlen_u8(s: Ptr[uint8]) -> uint64:
@@ -77,7 +77,7 @@
 
 
 # BSS scratch buffers — Adder quirks: module-scope BSS only.
-ls_buf: Array[65536, uint8]   # LISTBUF
+ls_buf: Array[4096, uint8]   # LISTBUF
 ls_modebuf: Array[12, uint8]
 
 
@@ -208,6 +208,6 @@
         # The listing filled the buffer, so entries past it were never read.
         # Say so and fail — a truncated listing reported as success is how a
         # user (or a script) concludes a directory is smaller than it is.
-        write_str(2, "ls: listing TRUNCATED at 65536 bytes — output is INCOMPLETE\n")
+        write_str(2, "ls: listing TRUNCATED at 4096 bytes — output is INCOMPLETE\n")
         return 1
     return 0
